          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %strong mere proposition
            is an internalization of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-truncated"} (-1)-truncated
            object. Mere propositions are also called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong h-propositions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-level"} h-level
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated+types"} truncated types
            %strong propositions
            if no ambiguity will result. Semantically, mere propositions correspond to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-groupoids"} (-1)-groupoids
            or more generally
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-truncated+objects"} (-1)-truncated objects
          %p
            Mere propositions are one way to embed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            into homotopy type theory, via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+some+types"} propositions as some types
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bracket+types"} bracket types
            approach. For many purposes, this is the “correct” way to embed logic (as opposed to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
            approach — for instance, the PAT version of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            contradicts the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sums"} dependent sums
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+products"} dependent products
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
          %p
            There are many equivalent definitions of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            being an
            = succeed "." do
              %em h-proposition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              a type, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi isProp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} isProp(A)
              denote the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
              for all pairs of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi isProp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} isProp(A) \coloneqq \prod_{x\colon A} \prod_{y\colon A} (x=y)
            %p
              We say that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is an h-proposition if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
